WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.317
Single ownership is the default in Rust,

2
00:00:02.397 --> 00:00:04.909
but some designs need shared ownership or
indirection.

3
00:00:04.989 --> 00:00:08.837
Smart pointers support these patterns
while still keeping responsibility visible.

4
00:00:08.917 --> 00:00:10.895
They are not escape hatches from
ownership.

5
00:00:10.975 --> 00:00:13.861
They are tools for expressing more
specific ownership designs.

6
00:00:13.941 --> 00:00:15.544
`Box<T>` gives owned indirection.

7
00:00:15.624 --> 00:00:17.815
It stores a value behind a stable owning
pointer,

8
00:00:17.895 --> 00:00:21.048
which can be useful for large values,
recursive types, trait objects,

9
00:00:21.128 --> 00:00:24.656
or cases where moving the pointer is
simpler than moving the underlying value.

10
00:00:24.736 --> 00:00:28.370
The box still has one owner, and cleanup
still happens when that owner is dropped.

11
00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:31.657
`Rc<T>` supports shared ownership within a
single-threaded context.

12
00:00:31.737 --> 00:00:33.714
Multiple owners can point to the same
value,

13
00:00:33.794 --> 00:00:36.199
and the value is cleaned up when the last
owner is gone.

14
00:00:36.279 --> 00:00:38.684
`Arc<T>` supports shared ownership across
threads.

15
00:00:38.764 --> 00:00:42.292
The distinction matters because
thread-safe sharing has different costs and

16
00:00:42.372 --> 00:00:44.403
constraints than single-threaded sharing.

17
00:00:44.483 --> 00:00:46.834
Shared ownership is not the same as shared
mutation.

18
00:00:46.914 --> 00:00:49.587
If multiple parts of the program need to
mutate shared data,

19
00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:53.354
the design needs controlled
synchronization or an interior mutability pattern.

20
00:00:53.434 --> 00:00:54.877
These tools can be appropriate,

21
00:00:54.957 --> 00:00:58.752
but they should be used deliberately
because they move some checks from compile time

22
00:00:58.832 --> 00:01:01.077
into runtime rules or synchronization
behavior.

23
00:01:01.157 --> 00:01:03.882
Threads and async tasks make ownership
lifetimes explicit.

24
00:01:03.962 --> 00:01:06.528
Data moved into a task must live long
enough for that task.

25
00:01:06.608 --> 00:01:09.707
Data shared across tasks must be safe to
share under the chosen model.

26
00:01:09.787 --> 00:01:13.101
Resource cleanup and cancellation behavior
should be part of the design.

27
00:01:13.181 --> 00:01:16.815
Ownership gives teams a language for
reasoning about these lifecycle questions

28
00:01:16.895 --> 00:01:19.140
before production behavior becomes
surprising.
